/**
 * English stopword list. Unlike German (where all nouns are capitalized),
 * English only capitalizes proper nouns - so the capitalization-based noun
 * heuristic from Stopwords::isStopword() doesn't work here. Instead, a large
 * negative list - function words, adverbs, adjectives, and especially verbs
 * in their correctly inflected forms - does most of the work.
 *
 * Verb inflection is NOT generated by naively appending suffixes (get+ing =
 * "geting" would be wrong), but rule-based via conjugate():
 *  - Final consonant doubling before -ing/-ed for monosyllabic verbs and
 *    verbs stressed on the final syllable (get -> getting, occur -> occurring)
 *  - Silent -e is dropped before -ing; for -ed, just "d" is appended
 *    (use -> using/used); exceptions like -ee/-oe/-ye keep the e
 *    (agree -> agreeing, dye -> dyeing)
 *  - Consonant+y -> "ies"/"ied" (try -> tries/tried), vowel+y stays unchanged
 *    (play -> plays/played)
 *  - -s/-x/-z/-ch/-sh -> "es" for third person (watch -> watches)
 *  - True irregular forms are listed explicitly (go -> went/gone), since
 *    these follow no rule
 *
 * Because inflection is rule-based, ongoing maintenance only ever requires
 * adding the *base form* of a new verb to VERB_BASES (or to IRREGULAR for
 * irregular inflection) - every word form is generated automatically.
 * That's the only maintenance this is designed to need.
 *
 * British English (verbs): rather than maintaining every form twice, it's
 * derived automatically from the American base form:
 *  - "-ize"/"-yze" -> "-ise"/"-yse" (realize -> realise, analyze -> analyse),
 *    including all inflected forms, automatically for EVERY verb with this
 *    ending
 *  - Verbs ending in vowel+"l" double the "l" before -ed/-ing in British
 *    English but not in American English (travel -> travelled/travelling
 *    BrE, traveled/traveling AmE) - handled via the short, explicit
 *    DUAL_L_VERBS list (the general doubling heuristic can't automate this,
 *    since both spellings are valid)
 *
 * British English (nouns/adjectives): these don't follow inflection rules,
 * so NOUN_ADJ_VARIANTS lists explicit American/British spelling pairs
 * (colour/color, centre/center, defence/defense, ...). Both the singular
 * and a regular plural ("+s") are generated automatically per pair.
 */
final class StopwordsEn
{
